  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nStratus was developing, mainly across NW PA, with low level\nmoisture in place. Model soundings and HREF probabilities of low\nclouds indicate this area should expand through the early\nmorning, and cover much of the area north of Rt 422, and along\nthe ridges. \n\nSurface low pressure is expected to track east across the Lower\nGreat Lakes and Ontario through the day. A tightening pressure\ngradient, a 30-25kt 850 mb jet, and mixing should result in\ngusty SW wind (around 25 mph) by late morning. This should\nresult in stratus mixing out and dissipating. Rising 500 mb\nheights and mixing should also result in high temperatures\naround 15 degrees above average. A morning shower is possible\nnorth of I-80 as the low tracks to our north.\n\nA shortwave is expected to advance eastward from the Midwest\nTuesday night and Wednesday, as a surface cold front drifts\nsouth across the Upper Ohio Valley region. Showers are expected\nto increase as this wave crosses the region. Shear increases,\nthough instability is expected to be minimal, with ML CAPE less\nthan 500 j/kg south of the cold front by Wednesday afternoon. A\nfew gusty thunderstorms are possible mainly south of I-70, where\nthe Storm Prediction Center has added a Marginal Risk for\nisolated damaging wind gusts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate the surface cold front should stall\nsouth of I-70 Wednesday night into Thursday, before returning\nnorth as a warm front Thursday afternoon as a deepening\nshortwave trough and surface low pressure track across the \nMidwest. The low is expected to reach the Lower Great Lakes\nregion late Thursday night into Friday, pulling a strong cold\nfront across the Upper Ohio Valley region. A jet aloft, mixing,\nand a strong pressure gradient should result in wind gusts from\n35-45 mph across the region Friday, with advisory level gusts\nlikely across portions of the higher terrain. There is a\npossibility of high wind warning criteria gusts across eastern\nTucker county. In the lower elevations, NBM probabilities for \ngusts of 45 mph or greater are generally 50 percent or less, \nthough these will be monitored over the next couple of days. \nBUFKIT momentum transfer does indicate a potential for advisory \nlevel wind gusts across the remainder of the area on Friday as \nwell. \n\nStrong cold advection after FROPA, an upper trough, and lake\nenhancement should result in rain and snow shower chances Friday\nnight into early Saturday. Probability of a tenth of an inch of\nsnow is generally 10 percent or less from NW PA to the higher\nterrain. Building surface high pressure should result in\ndecreasing rain/snow showers chances by Saturday afternoon. With\nthe colder air, frost and freeze conditions will also be\npossible, especially Saturday night.",
